1
00:00:01,060 --> 00:00:06,359
In the Atlantic Ocean, about a thousand miles
west of Africa, is Null Island. It's a busy

2
00:00:06,359 --> 00:00:10,799
place: Thousands of people live there, millions
of photos have been taken there, and occasionally,

3
00:00:10,799 --> 00:00:14,910
people teleport in for just a moment or two
before disappearing to somewhere else.

4
00:00:14,910 --> 00:00:18,910
Or at least, that's what it looks like to
a computer. Because if you actually set sail

5
00:00:18,910 --> 00:00:23,050
for Null Island, you’d find nothing but
deep blue ocean and a lonely weather buoy.

6
00:00:23,050 --> 00:00:27,140
Or boo-ey, if you’re American. There is
one special thing you might notice: it’s

7
00:00:27,140 --> 00:00:32,079
at exactly zero degrees longitude and zero
degrees latitude -- and that’s the key to

8
00:00:32,079 --> 00:00:36,270
why computers think this particular patch
of ocean is a busy place.

9
00:00:36,270 --> 00:00:41,790
Any big database, whether it's storing information
about people, photos, or cats, needs to know

10
00:00:41,790 --> 00:00:46,170
the difference between “nothing”, like
zero, and nothing, as in, there’s no data,

11
00:00:46,170 --> 00:00:51,620
it’s a mystery. That idea of “no data”
is called “NULL”. So here we know that

12
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:56,860
Ser Pounce has black fur, and Mr Sniffywinkles
has brown fur, but we don’t know about Schrödinger.

13
00:00:56,860 --> 00:01:01,580
NULL doesn’t mean “no colour” or “no
fur at all”. It literally means "there is

14
00:01:01,580 --> 00:01:05,299
no data here, we don’t know what color her
fur is".

15
00:01:05,299 --> 00:01:08,820
This isn’t just a cat-related problem. If
your phone doesn’t know where you are, it

16
00:01:08,820 --> 00:01:13,200
might tag a photo you take with latitude NULL
and longitude NULL, or it might tell an app

17
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:18,219
that your location is NULL, NULL. No problem
so far. But badly written apps can read that

18
00:01:18,219 --> 00:01:23,359
as co-ordinates “zero, zero”: they’ve
mistaken nothing for “nothing”. So they’ll

19
00:01:23,359 --> 00:01:28,850
think that you are on Null Island -- at least
until your phone works out where you really

20
00:01:28,850 --> 00:01:34,649
are. More seriously, in a 2012 US election,
many voters in Wisconsin lived in places that

21
00:01:34,649 --> 00:01:39,520
the Census Bureau didn’t have co-ordinates
for. So a new, automatic system said they

22
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:43,899
lived on Null Island… which is definitely
not in any Wisconsin election district. If

23
00:01:43,899 --> 00:01:47,999
the mistake hadn’t been caught in time,
they might have had problems voting.

24
00:01:47,999 --> 00:01:51,549
You can also get problems when you try to
put the actual word "null" into a database,

25
00:01:51,549 --> 00:01:55,700
like you might try to do if you’re one of
the 300,000+ people with “Null” as your

26
00:01:55,700 --> 00:02:00,590
last name. It only takes a small programming
error -- mistaking “Null” for NULL -- and

27
00:02:00,590 --> 00:02:04,060
suddenly, the computer thinks you don’t
exist, or at least you can't register an account

28
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:09,390
with your favorite kitty-litter-cleanup-service.
All this is a reminder that our world is complicated,

29
00:02:09,390 --> 00:02:13,140
and it’s not always as easy as you might
think to map our messy reality into a computer

30
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:17,549
system. So the next time you see a photo tagged
way out in the Atlantic Ocean, you’ll know

31
00:02:17,549 --> 00:02:23,489
what’s happened. Unless it really is a photo
of that one, lonely weather buoy. Boo-ey.

32
00:02:27,068 --> 00:02:31,628
thanks to Tom Scott of the amazing youtube
channel, Tom Scott, for helping write and

33
00:02:34,124 --> 00:02:36,904
brilliant other videos on his channel, Tom
Scott.